<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Matanzas</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Matanzas is a port city on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>, founded by the Spanish in 1693. Its bay was the scene of a celebrated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> triumph in September 1628, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piet-heyn">Piet Heyn</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> trapped the Spanish treasure fleet there and seized its cargo of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and goods. The city later grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> in the nineteenth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matanzas.jpg" alt="Matanzas" width="320" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jerome Ryan · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cuba_-_Matanzas_-_Matanzas_from_Ermita_de_Monserrate_church.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
